# F04 · After Agreement Ends: What Denial Can Change

[Seven essays](../README.md) · [Current selection](../../../docs/selection.md) · [After the midterm](../../../docs/post-midterm.md)

A father offers his son a pretzel, then denies having made a promise. The son disputes that explanation but accepts a different snack. Has the original claim disappeared, or has the son simply stopped pursuing it? The essay examines what this example can establish about the effect of denying a promise.

[Read R1](R1.md) · [Original draft](draft.md) · [Argument critique](critique-A.md) · [Literature critique](critique-B.md) · [Author response](author-response.md)

## What changed

The revision gives up the draft's attempted refutation of Clapp's general theory. It instead argues that his example does too little to distinguish cancellation of a claim from non-pursuit, negotiation, or limits on enforcing it. R1 acknowledges that Clapp supplies a rule linking conversational agreement to obligations, then questions its temporal interpretation and support. Close predecessors and counterevidence remain part of the case.

## Production record

A free investigation begun before the resource pause, completed through the [shared author–two-critics–revision cycle](../README.md#the-shared-writing-cycle). Both critics read the preserved original draft; a fresh editor produced the response and R1. Some working snapshots were reconstructed later and remain labeled as such.

**Recorded settings:** Astra/max draft; GPT-6.1 Sol/high critics; Sol/xhigh editor; Standard continuation.

